#!/bin/sh
# PATH shim for the Codex CLI. Every terminal `codex` comes through here,
# enables the startup update check, and execs the vendor binary.
#
# WHY IT IS ON. The check records whether a newer version exists. It never
# installs one in Lody's headless `codex app-server` process. The payload-owned
# agent-cli-update service runs the explicit `codex update` command that does
# install it. This shim states the check value so a vendor default change does
# not silently disable version discovery.
#
# WHY A CONFIG OVERRIDE AND NOT AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE: codex has no
# environment variable for this. The setting is a config key, so `-c key=value`
# is the only per-process way to set it. Checked against @openai/codex@0.147.0:
# `codex doctor --json` reports `"check for update on startup": "true"` by
# default, and honours either value passed this way.
#
# WHAT AN EXPLICIT UPDATE DOES: it rewrites the package in NPM_CONFIG_PREFIX,
# which is /opt/blitz/npm. /usr/local/bin stays ahead of that prefix on PATH,
# so this shim execs the updated binary.
#
# WHY THE FLAG GOES FIRST: codex accepts `-c` more than once and the last one
# wins, so a caller that really wants a different value keeps the last word.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS FATAL. The flag is an ordinary argument and codex ignores a
# config key it does not know, so a future version that drops the key still
# starts.

exec /opt/blitz/npm/bin/codex -c check_for_update_on_startup=true "$@"
